3. --legacy-peer-deps

Peer dependencies are packages that another package expects you to install yourself (e.g., React plugins needing a matching version of React).

By default, newer versions of npm (v7+) enforce strict rules and will throw errors if peer dependencies conflict.

Using this flag:

npm ignores peer dependency conflicts.

It installs the dependencies the old (pre-npm v7) way.

Useful when:

You have older packages that don’t declare peer dependencies correctly.

You just want the installation to succeed without fighting version conflicts.

Downside: You might end up with incompatible versions that cause runtime errors.

✅ In short:
npm install --ignore-scripts --legacy-peer-deps
installs your dependencies while skipping install scripts and ignoring peer dependency conflicts.

